Carafe Lab: Eden's OpenGL renderer binds a small buffer of zeros to every vertex binding before it binds the
first vertex buffers, so an active attribute whose stream was never set reads zeros from a buffer, as on the
console. Eden draws with the default vertex array object of a compatibility context, where an enabled attribute
on a binding with no buffer is a client-memory array: Mesa then mapped the index buffer on every indexed draw to
find the vertex range, which waits for the GPU to finish all its work (Hitman 2: Silent Assassin, NVK's shaders
read 16 attributes while DXVK sets 4 streams, ran at a third of its speed once buffer uploads stopped being lost).
GPL-3.0-or-later, as the files it changes.

